This project involved designing and building an autonomous cube-collecting robot for MAE 3780 Mechatronics. The robot was designed to navigate a game board using QTI edge sensors and a color sensor while collecting cubes through a front intake mechanism. Our original design included a one-way flap collection system, ultrasonic sensing for cube detection, and larger wheels for additional clearance, but several mechanical and electrical limitations forced us to iteratively redesign the robot throughout development.

As we tested the robot, we encountered challenges with sensor reliability, wheel integration, and mechanical intake design. The hinged collection system proved too stiff for cubes to pass through efficiently, so we replaced it with extended cardboard intake arms and adjusted the navigation logic to prioritize forward-only movement. We also attempted to integrate an ultrasonic sensor for object detection, but power limitations and time constraints ultimately led us to simplify the system and focus on more reliable sensor-based navigation.

The final robot used QTI sensors for boundary detection and a color sensor for board navigation, with embedded C code controlling motor movement and recovery behavior. During competition, varying lighting conditions significantly impacted QTI sensor performance, requiring us to quickly adapt the robot by relying more heavily on color sensing. Although the robot did not fully perform as intended, the project provided valuable experience in embedded systems, iterative engineering design, debugging under constraints, and integrating mechanical, electrical, and software systems into a functioning autonomous robot.

